Acquisition SDK progress: the [SDK lock](../configs/lumi-vm-acquisition-sdk-v0.1.lock.json)
reuses five versions and hashes from the existing project staging lock. Public
package metadata and the actual five wheels agree: 15,791,700 wheel bytes were
downloaded and SHA-256 verified. The first fetch failed because this Python's
default trust store had zero CA certificates (verification error 20). The fresh
v0.2 fetch uses an existing hash-bound certifi bundle, keeping certificate and
hostname verification enabled. The failed v0.1 attempt remains untouched;
neither attempt automatically retries.

A fresh SDK-only local venv was installed offline with required hashes. Its
dependency check and all four existing wire checks pass using the unchanged
production SDK factory, the standard SDK rather than the AWS CLI vendor alias,
dummy credentials and doubled HTTP sends. The regular suite now passes 318
checks (303 previous plus 15 SDK preparation/TLS tests), with zero network or
payload guard violations during those checks. This turn did use public package
networking; it did not read AWS credentials or S3 objects. These results are on
Mac/Python 3.13.7, **not native Linux/Python 3.12 qualification**. The next runtime
work is offline installation and qualification of these pinned wheels on the
concrete Linux host. Host admission, credential teardown, approved off-host
retention and the real full-size import remain open.

Offline Linux setup is now implemented, not executed: the
[installer](../scripts/install_sft_vm_acquisition_sdk_v1.py) consumes an exact
11-member, 15,851,520-byte bundle containing the five verified wheels and the
unchanged SDK-check source closure. It creates `/opt/lumi-sft-acquisition/venv`
