Concrete host preparation: [the full-size host proposal](lumi-vm-fullsize-host-run-proposal-v0.1.md)
now selects one `r6i.4xlarge` (16 vCPU, 128 GiB), the same available Ubuntu image,
encrypted 32-GiB root plus 128-GiB work volumes, and a four-hour whole-host
ceiling. Read-only AWS identity, image, offering, subnet, quota and price checks
succeeded on 2026-09-28. Compute is $1.008/hour at that observation; known
four-hour compute/storage is about $4.10 before ancillary charges. The proposed
$10 active-run budget is not a hard AWS billing cap. Preserved volumes would
add about $0.421/day until approved cleanup.

No host was launched. Both disks are proposed as non-auto-deleting to preserve
real output; expiry must stop compute even if backup fails. The proposal is
explicitly blocked on three integration outcomes: executable root host/bootstrap
admission, acquisition credential teardown before import, and bounded private
backup with independent readback/lifecycle. Native SDK installation alone would
not close those gaps. The former tiny-run approval does not authorize this run.

Credential handoff implementation update: the
[new root-parent join](../src/lumi/posttraining_vm_fullsize_credential_handoff_v1.py)
now calls the unchanged acquisition parent, removes only the authenticated root
credential source after empty-cgroup proof, unmounts its private tmpfs without
force/lazy flags, fsyncs an aggregate cleanup receipt, then calls the unchanged
completion path. It preserves acquisition's successful exited state because
the frozen handoff must validate and stop that unit itself before import.
This avoids breaking the existing terminal gate. File unlink/unmount is not a
claim of cryptographic erasure or AWS credential revocation.

All 350 selected local checks pass (332 prior plus 18 new). Tests exercise real
tiny-file acquisition handoff and source unlink; root ownership, Linux mounts,
systemd operations and completion are doubled. Changed source/mount identity,
live children, failed unlink/unmount or failed cleanup receipt prevent import.
Failed attempts preserve aggregate records and do not retry cleanup. No real
credential, model payload, AWS resource or native mount was accessed or changed.

The cleanup **code path is now connected locally**; host bootstrap must still
provision the root-only nonshared tmpfs with host swap disabled and properly
scoped short-lived credentials, bind this successor in the final release and
qualify it natively. Existing source bundles are unchanged and do not include
the new module yet. The remaining execution integration is that admitted root
bootstrap plus private backup/readback/lifecycle, followed by final bindings
and bounded full-size approval. Public execution entry points remain closed.

Credential provisioning is now joined locally by the
[private root setup driver](../src/lumi/posttraining_vm_fullsize_credential_setup_v1.py).
It creates and verifies the private 128-KiB tmpfs, reads at most 64 KiB once from
a root-owned private pipe within five seconds, validates the temporary-credential
shape/expiry, writes only the existing three credential fields, then invokes
the already tested acquisition/cleanup workflow. No secret is passed in argv,
environment or aggregate reports. A partial write is removable only when its
inode was created by this invocation; unexpected files or ambiguous mounts
are preserved for host-level cleanup. A preflight failure before the workflow's
exclusive marker does not falsely transfer cleanup responsibility.

The driver requires 3,675–7,200 seconds of credential validity at dispatch to
cover the existing 3,615-second acquisition ceiling and a 60-second margin.
A default one-hour session will fail closed; the future credential issuer must
support the required lifetime or a separately bounded change is needed. This
is **shape/expiry validation, not authentication of the issuer or IAM scope**.
No AWS session was issued, real secret read, mount performed or host launched.

All 366 selected local checks pass (350 prior plus 16 new); real synthetic pipes
and temporary files are exercised while native mounts/ownership/systemd and
completion remain doubled. Root host/runtime/source/disk/egress/expiry admission,
authenticated least-privilege credential delivery and private backup/readback
still need integration and native acceptance. The complete cloud launcher is
not finished; existing source bundles remain unchanged and omit these new
root-driver modules until the final authenticated release is assembled.

The [live credential-issuer check](../evidence/lumi-vm-fullsize-credential-issuer-observation-v0.1.json)
on 2026-09-28 found that both the default and `lumi-s3` CLI profiles resolve to
the account root identity. Login is valid. Resolving `lumi-s3-canary` caused its
configured role assumption to fail with AWS `AccessDenied`: root accounts may
not assume roles. No temporary session was issued by that failed probe.
The complete `/lumi/` role-path listing contains 13 roles: two trust only EC2,
11 trust only Glue, and all report a 3,600-second maximum session duration.
This is not an account-wide issuer inventory or a permissions-policy audit.
No compatible direct issuer has been identified for the unchanged private-pipe
contract. A non-root controller login is needed before checking the concrete
trust, exact source permissions and achievable session duration; it alone does
not close host admission. IAM mutations and any credential-duration or
instance-profile contract changes require explicit approval. Root credentials
must not be delivered to the VM. No import was retried, IAM changed, payload
accessed, instance launched or GPU used in this check.

The user subsequently authorized temporary IAM access with cleanup. A
[live deny-all issuer check](../evidence/lumi-vm-temporary-issuer-verification-v0.1.json)
demonstrated the non-root route: create a narrowly scoped temporary user/key,
assume its dedicated role for 7,200 seconds, then remove all newly created IAM
resources. The returned session had 7,199 seconds remaining, satisfying the
unchanged lifetime requirement. Both the role and session explicitly denied
all resource actions; there were no S3 or instance requests. The user/key, two
inline policies and role were deleted. Independent post-process GetUser/GetRole
checks confirmed absence. No credential is retained for subsequent work.

The initial v0.1 probe stopped before IAM creation because the CLI stdin JSON
adapter was rejected. Its code and evidence remain unchanged; v0.2 corrected
only that adapter, passed a read-only identity precheck, and completed the live
issuance/cleanup check. Eleven synthetic checks and Ruff pass. This establishes
a way to bootstrap a non-root issuer without asking the user to create a new
login; it is not exact-source permission verification, authenticated delivery,
native host admission or a full-size run. Those need fresh just-in-time scoped
credentials after the remaining workflow is bound. No new login is currently
requested from the user. The old root/profile observations remain historical
evidence, not the current blocker by themselves.

